    <!--
        AccessContentBuilder ("Access drag-and-drop content builder") + CreateLtngTempInPub
        ("Manage Public Lightning Email Templates") are the pair needed to build AND edit a
        Lightning (SFX) email template in Content Builder. AccessContentBuilder alone opens the
        Builder; without CreateLtngTempInPub the Save button fails once the template lives in a
        SHARED folder (IR_Email_Templates_Lightning), because a shared-folder template counts as
        "public" for this permission's purposes. AccessContentBuilder was already granted in the
        org (reconciled 2026-08-15) and is added here so a future deploy of this file does not
        silently wipe it; see ARCHITECTURE.md's PermissionSet REPLACE-not-merge warning.
        CreateLtngTempInPub was net-new in both the org and this file before this change.

        EmailTemplateManagement ("Manage Email Templates", added 2026-08-15) is the narrow,
        purpose-built permission (confirmed via live PermissionSet describe) for viewing,
        creating, editing and deleting email templates and folders in Setup, WITHOUT needing the
        much broader CustomizeApplication permission already on this set. It is added because it
        is the CORRECT scoped grant for this feature going forward — CustomizeApplication is left
        untouched per instruction, but this set should not depend on it for email-template access
        specifically. CreateLtngTempFolder ("Create Folders for Lightning Email Templates") was
        considered and deliberately NOT added: the requirement is to create/edit TEMPLATES inside
        the existing shared IR_Email_Templates_Lightning folder, not to create new folders.
        EmailTemplate `objectPermissions` (allowRead/allowCreate/allowEdit) was verified via a
        real check-only dry-run deploy to be a structurally valid PermissionSet element at API
        67.0 (it deployed with zero errors) — but a live org-wide query found ZERO existing
        ObjectPermissions rows for EmailTemplate across every permission set and profile in this
        org, and the documented access model for this object is the special userPermissions above
        rather than object CRUD, so it was deliberately NOT added here to avoid an unverified,
        unprecedented grant with no evidence it does anything.
    -->
    <userPermissions>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
        <name>AccessContentBuilder</name>
    </userPermissions>
